<UserControl x:Class="Barista.Views.DonePage"
             xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
             xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml">
  <StackPanel>
    <TextBlock Style="{StaticResource H1}" Text="Installed. Now the careful part."/>

    <Border x:Name="RebootCard" Style="{StaticResource CardBorder}"
            Background="#EDF4EF" BorderBrush="{StaticResource Good}">
      <StackPanel>
        <TextBlock FontWeight="SemiBold" Foreground="{StaticResource Good}"
                   Text="Every stage ran, and the machine came back after each one"/>
        <TextBlock Margin="0,6,0,0" MaxWidth="640">
          Each stage that registers something LSA reads at boot got its own restart, and you
          confirmed the machine was healthy before the next one went in. Whatever is installed
          is loaded now.
        </TextBlock>
      </StackPanel>
    </Border>

    <Border Style="{StaticResource CardBorder}">
      <StackPanel>
        <TextBlock FontWeight="SemiBold" Text="Loaded is not the same as called"/>
        <TextBlock Margin="0,6,0,0" MaxWidth="640">
          Microsoft documents the Auth0 filter slot for domain controllers. Whether MSV1_0 calls
          it for local accounts on a workstation is the open question from Part 2, and nothing in
          this installer can answer it. Attach a kernel debugger, make a network logon for an
          enrolled account, and watch for:
        </TextBlock>
        <TextBlock Margin="0,10,0,0" FontFamily="Consolas" FontSize="12"
                   Foreground="{StaticResource Muted}"><Run Text="[Doppio-SubAuth] ..."/><LineBreak/><Run Text="[Ristretto] ..."/></TextBlock>
        <TextBlock Margin="0,10,0,0" MaxWidth="640" Foreground="{StaticResource Muted}">
          No lines means the filter is loaded and never asked. Everything it was supposed to
          enforce is then not enforced, and only the deny rights are doing anything.
        </TextBlock>
      </StackPanel>
    </Border>

    <Border x:Name="EnrollCard" Style="{StaticResource CardBorder}" Visibility="Collapsed">
      <StackPanel>
        <TextBlock FontWeight="SemiBold" Text="When a code is refused"/>
        <TextBlock Margin="0,6,0,10" MaxWidth="640">
          The likeliest thing to go wrong from here is not a bugcheck, it is a locked account
          after a few wrong codes — and with the tile as the only sign-in option that looks
          exactly like a broken tile. From an elevated prompt, next to TacProvider.dll:
        </TextBlock>
        <TextBlock FontFamily="Consolas" FontSize="12" Foreground="{StaticResource Muted}"><Run Text="enroll.exe /unlock alice   clear a lockout from wrong codes"/><LineBreak/><Run Text="enroll.exe /list           who is enrolled"/><LineBreak/><Run Text="enroll.exe alice           re-enrol (issues a NEW secret)"/><LineBreak/><Run Text="enroll.exe /remove alice   drop one account's secret"/></TextBlock>
        <TextBlock Margin="0,10,0,0" MaxWidth="640" Foreground="{StaticResource Muted}">
          Re-enrolling replaces the secret, which silently invalidates whatever is already in the
          authenticator app. Unlock first; re-enrol only if you mean to scan a new code. A clock
          more than a step out of sync looks like a wrong code too.
        </TextBlock>
      </StackPanel>
    </Border>

    <Border x:Name="GateCard" Style="{StaticResource CardBorder}" Visibility="Collapsed">
      <StackPanel>
        <TextBlock FontWeight="SemiBold" Text="Using the gate"/>
        <TextBlock Margin="0,6,0,10" MaxWidth="640">
          From an elevated prompt on this machine, next to TacProvider.dll:
        </TextBlock>
        <TextBlock FontFamily="Consolas" FontSize="12" Foreground="{StaticResource Muted}"><Run Text="ristretto alice            open the gate (asks for a code)"/><LineBreak/><Run Text="ristretto /status alice    how long is left"/><LineBreak/><Run Text="ristretto /close alice     close it now"/><LineBreak/><Run Text="ristretto /mode off        kill switch, no reboot"/></TextBlock>
        <TextBlock Margin="0,10,0,0" MaxWidth="640" Foreground="{StaticResource Muted}">
          While a gate is open, anyone who can authenticate as that account gets in — not only
          the person who opened it. Close it when you are done.
        </TextBlock>
      </StackPanel>
    </Border>

    <Border Style="{StaticResource CardBorder}" Background="{StaticResource DangerBg}"
            BorderBrush="{StaticResource Danger}">
      <StackPanel>
        <TextBlock FontWeight="SemiBold" Foreground="{StaticResource Danger}"
                   Text="When you are finished with this VM"/>
        <TextBlock Margin="0,6,0,12" MaxWidth="640">
          Put RunAsPPL back to 1 and reboot. Leaving lsass unprotected is the one change here
          that keeps costing you something after the experiment is over.
        </TextBlock>
        <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal">
          <Button x:Name="PplButton" Content="Turn RunAsPPL back on" Click="OnRestorePpl"
                  Margin="0,0,10,0"/>
          <Button Content="Uninstall everything" Style="{StaticResource DangerButton}"
                  Click="OnUninstall"/>
        </StackPanel>
        <TextBlock x:Name="UninstallLog" Margin="0,12,0,0" FontFamily="Consolas" FontSize="11.5"
                   Foreground="{StaticResource Muted}" Visibility="Collapsed"/>
      </StackPanel>
    </Border>
  </StackPanel>
</UserControl>
